## OPERATING RULES (every task)
- `roadmap` → print the READY map and WAIT. `go <id>` → that ONE task. Several ids → take the first, say the rest need parallel sessions. Whoever merges updates the map.
- Own branch `task/<id>` or, if another session is live in this checkout, YOUR OWN WORKTREE (`git worktree add`); never move the `main` ref from a worktree others commit into; `git branch --show-current` before every commit (CLAUDE.md).
- Merge evidence = the task's GATES as numbers in a handoff (compressed into the task's block below, then the block is deleted when the id is done — DECISIONS keeps the verdict) + `npm run typecheck && npm test` green + the named oracles green.
- FROZEN RULE (Robert 2026-07-21): never break a working path; every new engine ships capability-gated with the current path as runtime fallback; defaults move only on Robert's yes. Frozen constraints: instant record start · instant default export · no idle device access · no streaming as the capture basis (the local full-quality file is always the capture; streaming/shipping is an extra output) · src/core never imports src/app · contracts in src/core/types.ts · NO take-length cap.
- Behaviour a user sees or hears changes only with Robert's yes; the engine never refuses a press; capacity knowledge may shape a take UP, never down.
- THE CEILING RULING (Robert 2026-09-02, DECISIONS robert (7)): the target is the platform's MAXIMUM and then past it — "MVP" is never a reason to scope a capability down. A capability that runs only on top hardware still SHIPS: capability-gated, elastic fallback underneath, never rejected for being unreachable on weak machines. Native (P4) is the direction, not a "later", because the browser's walls are the present ceiling.
- EVERY MOVEMENT IS KEPT (Robert 2026-09-02, DECISIONS robert (8)): in any change-detection path, whatever changed at all is kept — no threshold, no "too small to matter" heuristic. Only the user decides what is irrelevant. Binds ideas 1/16, X17's census and AI1's picker.
- A gate you cannot meet = stop and report WHY with measurements. A failed spike with data is a success; code without evidence is not. No refactors outside scope.
- HEAVY RUNS GO THROUGH `scripts/gate.sh` — `scripts/gate.sh npm run oracle`, `scripts/gate.sh npm run exp -- pressure`. One heavy run at a time across every session and every worktree (the lock lives in the common git dir). NOT because of git, which T1 fixed, but because these are TIMING gates on an 8 GB machine: two sessions rendering at once do not take twice as long, they take each other's numbers down, and G6 is a whole task about gates that flake under load. The failure mode this prevents is the expensive one — a session reads a red gate that is red because ANOTHER session was rendering, believes it, and "fixes" a bug that does not exist. Cheap commands (typecheck, unit tests) need no lock. `scripts/gate.sh --status` says who holds it; a holder whose process died is reaped automatically.
- LEAVE A RESUME SNAPSHOT: `.ai/wip/<id>.md`, rewritten (never appended) as the task moves and committed on the task branch with everything else. Five headings, no prose: DONE (with the numbers already measured) · NEXT (the single next action, concrete) · MEASURED (every number this task has produced, so nobody re-runs a rig to learn what is already known) · BLOCKED (what it is waiting on, and whose it is) · DO NOT (dead ends already paid for). A session that stops mid-task — usage limit, context, a closed laptop — leaves its worktree and branch intact; the snapshot is what lets the next session pick it up without re-deriving. Delete the file in the commit that lands the task.
- A SESSION THAT RUNS OUT MID-TASK HAS NOT FAILED. Its commits are on its own branch, the push gate keeps a broken commit local, and nothing reaches main without the task's gates. Take the work as far as it goes, keep the snapshot current, and stop cleanly — never rush a gate to "finish", and never widen scope to use up remaining room.
- Questions to Robert go through the AskUserQuestion UI, one at a time, recommended option first. Docs are for agents: current truth, compressed; done items deleted on sight.
- Anything touching capture/* keeps BOTH engines green: `npm run oracle` (v2) AND `node scripts/oracle.mjs --engine=v1`. Anything touching the STOP path also runs `npm run oracle:load` (HEAVY, announce) and `npm run exp -- armcancel`.
- LONG CELL MANDATORY before any flip touching the instant or smart-cut paths: `npm run oracle:long` (~3 min, HEAVY). Quote instant/trimmed numbers against the baseline block below; a flip may not move them up.
- HEAVY RUNS announced, never while Robert uses the machine (8 GB M3). Read a red gate against HEAD run cold beside it; the known flaky gates are G6's.
- HIDDEN-PANE RULE: the agent browser pane is a hidden document (timers clamp to ~1 Hz) → never a rate, fps, elastic or timing test; use the headed scripts.
- Verify on prod (https://inout-kappa.vercel.app), not from the source; bust the service worker after a deploy (CLAUDE.md).
- BUG PACK: G lane (instrument defects) outranks B lane (user defects); evidence lives in BACKLOG.md.
- Conflict rules: the export has THREE paths in order — instant packet copy · smart cut · render (worker; no Window-only globals or every export silently takes the slow lane); every compose/* change says which path it touched · composite geometry lives in compose/layout.ts, capture/compositor.worker.ts, capture/liveComposite.ts and must agree; cameraTrack.ts owns the PiP · F3's frame is an EDIT (render only) · a picture change (colour, text, codec) and any audio attenuation are Robert's calls.

